            {
              "id": "5bd42fdf-e1f0-320e-a23a-395db8b1cda0",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure or deprecated C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In the given code snippet, the vulnerability does not seem to be directly related to a prohibited C function, but rather to a potential integer overflow issue.\n\nIf `numDevices` is 0, then `numDevices - 1` will result in a negative number, which can lead to unexpected behavior if used as an array index or loop boundary.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this type of vulnerability, it's important to always validate and sanitize inputs, and to use secure alternatives to deprecated or insecure C functions. In this case, you should ensure that `numDevices` is greater than 0 before subtracting 1 from it.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the given code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nif (numDevices > 0) {\n    src < numDevices - 1\n}\n```\n\nThis ensures that `numDevices - 1` will not result in a negative number.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe given code snippet does not appear to require any specific library dependencies.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are valid and active at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "931b3ea3-191e-3306-8e75-10edf555689b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Found\" is a vulnerability that occurs when a C++ program uses a function that is considered unsafe due to its potential to cause buffer overflows, memory corruption, or other security issues. These functions are often part of the C Standard Library and are included for backwards compatibility, but their use is discouraged in modern programming.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the vulnerability sink is `ev.outputToCsv`. Without more context, it's hard to say exactly what the issue is, but if `outputToCsv` is a function that writes to a file or sends data over a network, it could potentially be used to execute a buffer overflow attack or other form of exploitation.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using unsafe C functions in your C++ code. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ Standard Library. For example, instead of using `strcpy`, use `std::string`. Instead of `sprintf`, use `std::ostringstream`. \n\nIf you must use a potentially unsafe function, make sure to validate and sanitize all inputs to the function. Never trust user input without validation.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nWithout more context, it's hard to provide a specific fix. However, if `outputToCsv` is a function that writes to a file, a safer alternative might be to use the C++ file I/O functions:\n\n```cpp\n#include <fstream>\n\n// ...\n\nstd::ofstream file(\"output.csv\");\nif (file.is_open()) {\n    file << ev;\n    file.close();\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nWithout more context, it's hard to say what library dependencies are required. However, if the `outputToCsv` function is part of a library, that library would be a dependency. The C++ Standard Library is also a likely dependency.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are subject to change and may not be accessible to all users."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "fba98b0f-3327-3d1b-a2c4-ebb1d5050b00",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure or unsafe C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. These functions are often prohibited because they do not perform bounds checking or other necessary validation, making them susceptible to exploitation.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, there is no direct use of a prohibited C function. However, the comparison operation could potentially lead to logical errors or unexpected behavior if not properly handled.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate such vulnerabilities, it is recommended to:\n\n- Avoid using prohibited C functions. Instead, use safer alternatives that perform necessary validation checks.\n- Always validate and sanitize input data.\n- Use secure coding practices and follow coding standards.\n- Regularly perform code reviews and use static code analysis tools to detect potential vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe provided code snippet is a comparison operation and does not directly involve any prohibited C function. However, if `dst` and `numCpuDevices` are results of some function calls or calculations, it is recommended to check the return values and handle any errors appropriately. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif (dst == numCpuDevices) {\n    if (dst != 0) {\n        // Proceed with the operation\n    } else {\n        // Handle the error\n    }\n} else {\n    // Handle the error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet does not require any specific library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the provided links are active and accessible for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c7509b5a-ac08-39f2-91d4-af6a9780d3db",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other problems. These functions are often prohibited in secure coding standards. In the provided code snippet, the function `outputToCsv` is flagged as potentially dangerous.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to avoid using prohibited C functions. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ Standard Library or other trusted libraries. Always validate and sanitize input data before processing it. Also, consider using automated tools to detect and prevent the use of prohibited functions.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nWithout the full context of the code, it's hard to provide a specific fix. However, if `outputToCsv` is a custom function that uses prohibited C functions internally, you should revise its implementation to avoid them. If it's a prohibited function itself, you should replace it with a safer alternative.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe specific library dependencies required by the code example cannot be determined from the provided snippet. However, if `outputToCsv` is a function from a specific library, that library would be a dependency.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Proactive Controls](https://owasp.org/www-project-proactive-controls/)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
