              "id": "9b2d6eeb-2690-323c-af21-a1101d093c73",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Found\" is a vulnerability that occurs when a C++ program uses a function that is considered unsafe due to its potential to cause buffer overflows, memory corruption, or other security issues. These functions are often part of the C Standard Library and are included for backwards compatibility, but their use is discouraged in modern programming.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the vulnerability sink is `ev.outputToCsv`. Without more context, it's hard to say exactly what the issue is, but if `outputToCsv` is a function that writes to a file or sends data over a network, it could potentially be used to execute a buffer overflow attack or other form of exploitation.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using unsafe C functions in your C++ code. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ Standard Library. For example, instead of using `strcpy`, use `std::string`. Instead of `sprintf`, use `std::ostringstream`. \n\nIf you must use a potentially unsafe function, make sure to validate and sanitize all inputs to the function. Never trust user input without validation.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nWithout more context, it's hard to provide a specific fix. However, if `outputToCsv` is a function that writes to a file, a safer alternative might be to use the C++ file I/O functions:\n\n```cpp\n#include <fstream>\n\n// ...\n\nstd::ofstream file(\"output.csv\");\nif (file.is_open()) {\n    file << ev;\n    file.close();\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nWithout more context, it's hard to say what library dependencies are required. However, if the `outputToCsv` function is part of a library, that library would be a dependency. The C++ Standard Library is also a likely dependency.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are subject to change and may not be accessible to all users."
