              "id": "829c3176-fa4a-3786-a88f-bb2c48f19f17",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in this code snippet is related to the `strlen` function in C++. The `strlen` function returns the length of a string, excluding the null-terminating character. If the string is empty, `strlen` will return 0. In the code snippet, `strlen(argv[2])-1` is used as an index to access an element in the string. If `argv[2]` is an empty string, `strlen(argv[2])-1` will be -1, which is an invalid index and will lead to undefined behavior, potentially causing a buffer underflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always check the length of a string before using it as an index. If the string is empty, you should handle this case separately to avoid accessing an invalid index.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nif (strlen(argv[2]) > 0) {\n    units = argv[2][strlen(argv[2])-1];\n} else {\n    // Handle the case where argv[2] is an empty string\n}\n```\n\nIn this fixed version, we first check if `argv[2]` is an empty string by checking if `strlen(argv[2])` is greater than 0. If it is, we can safely use `strlen(argv[2])-1` as an index. If it is not, we handle the case where `argv[2]` is an empty string separately.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code snippet requires the following library to execute properly:\n\n- cstring\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Underflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-129: Improper Validation of Array Index](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/129.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
