            {
              "id": "c93f820b-feae-3eb1-82f3-bf51abf06d7e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the `vsnprintf` function is used, which can lead to buffer overflow if not used correctly.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions. In C++, the Standard Template Library (STL) provides many safe alternatives. For example, instead of using `vsnprintf`, you can use `std::string` and `std::stringstream` for string formatting and manipulation.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer alternative using `std::stringstream`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <sstream>\n#include <stdarg.h>\n\nvoid safeFunction(const char* format, ...) {\n    va_list args;\n    va_start(args, format);\n    std::stringstream ss;\n    while (*format != '\\0') {\n        if (*format == '%') {\n            format++;\n            if (*format == 'd') {\n                int i = va_arg(args, int);\n                ss << i;\n            }\n            else if (*format == 'c') {\n                // note automatic conversion to integral type\n                int c = va_arg(args, int);\n                ss << static_cast<char>(c);\n            }\n            else if (*format == 'f') {\n                double d = va_arg(args, double);\n                ss << d;\n            }\n            // add more specifiers as needed\n        }\n        else {\n            ss << *format;\n        }\n        format++;\n    }\n    va_end(args);\n    this->errMsg = ss.str();\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- `<sstream>`: for `std::stringstream`\n- `<stdarg.h>`: for `va_list`, `va_start`, and `va_arg`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ab5773bf-faf0-34f1-979f-3df38b085179",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Found\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. These functions are often prohibited in secure coding standards due to their inherent risks.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the function `atoll()` is used, which converts a string to a long long integer. This function does not perform any error checking, and if the input string is not a valid number, it can lead to undefined behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives that perform error checking. In C++, this can be achieved by using the `std::stoll()` function from the `<string>` library, which throws an exception if the conversion cannot be performed.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string>\n#include <cstdlib>\n\ntry {\n    numBytesPerTransfer = argc > 2 ? std::stoll(argv[2]) : DEFAULT_BYTES_PER_TRANSFER;\n} catch (const std::invalid_argument& ia) {\n    // Handle the exception\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdlib>`: For the `std::atoll()` function.\n- `<string>`: For the `std::stoll()` function.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are subject to change and may not always be accessible."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "83147a10-ded2-35d3-ad25-df6f3744332c",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other problems. These functions are often prohibited in secure coding standards. The use of such functions can lead to serious security vulnerabilities including arbitrary code execution, memory corruption, and denial-of-service attacks.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to avoid using dangerous C functions and instead use safer alternatives provided by the C++ standard library. These alternatives often include bounds checking or other mechanisms to prevent common vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nFor the specific vulnerability sink `!showMinOnly`, it's hard to provide a specific fix without the context of the code. However, if `showMinOnly` is a function that uses prohibited C functions, you should replace these with safer alternatives. For example, if `showMinOnly` uses `strcpy`, you could replace it with `strncpy` or even better, use C++ strings and their methods.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nWithout the specific code, it's hard to list the exact library dependencies. However, in general, C++ programs might require the following libraries:\n\n- iostream\n- string\n- vector\n- map\n- etc.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
