            {
              "id": "ec16e534-aa28-30b6-9f54-86623ac9730f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other problems. These functions are often prohibited in secure coding standards. In the provided code snippet, the function `vsnprintf` is used, which is generally safe, but can lead to vulnerabilities if not used correctly.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions whenever possible. For instance, instead of using `vsnprintf`, consider using `snprintf` with a maximum size for the output string. This can help prevent buffer overflow vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer alternative using `snprintf`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdio>\n#include <cstdarg>\n\nvoid safe_printf(const char* format, ...) {\n    va_list args;\n    va_start(args, format);\n    char buffer[256];\n    vsnprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), format, args);\n    va_end(args);\n    printf(\"%s\", buffer);\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdio>`: for `vsnprintf` and `printf`\n- `<cstdarg>`: for `va_list`, `va_start`, and `va_end`\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
